{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Generally, no. A ’40 Act ETF’s portfolio is held by the fund (typically with a custodian), not owned by its separate adviser or sponsor. The Investment Company Act requires fund assets to be maintained with a qualified custodian, and the fund is a distinct legal entity. So sponsor insolvency alone ordinarily does not make the portfolio property of the sponsor’s creditors or cancel shareholders’ fund interests.\n\nThat is asset separation, not a guarantee against every bad outcome. The result can depend on the fund’s legal structure, custody and recordkeeping, contractual arrangements, and the facts of an insolvency; fraud, commingling, operational disruption, litigation, or a fund’s own insolvency can still cause losses or delays. Shares represent an interest in the fund, not a direct claim to particular portfolio securities."}}
